The ADR recommends **Redis Streams**. The core argument is operational fit: the team of six has no Kafka experience and no infrastructure engineer, making a self-hosted Kafka deployment unsafe within the two-week deadline. Redis Streams already runs on existing infrastructure, handles the 500 req/s peak (and 10× growth) with ample headroom, and provides sufficient ordering and consumer-group semantics. Exactly-once for billing is handled via application-level idempotency using Redis `SET NX`, which the team can implement and review quickly. Kafka is acknowledged as technically superior in retention, exactly-once, and ecosystem maturity, but rejected because its operational burden is disproportionate to the team's constraints.
